    note "A new program, not saved yet"
    editor (5 lines, 1 marked, at 5:2, colours nexalang-horizon)
      | "fn main() {"
      | "let total = 1"
      | "total += 41"
      | "print(\"The answer is {total}\")"
      | "}"
    list "No mistakes found." (1 rows)
      - "Line 3: can't change `total`: it was made with `let` (or is a parameter)"
    code (25 lines)
      | "About this mistake: Changing something that can't change"
      | ""
      | "`let` makes a value that stays the same, `var` one that can change."
      | "Most values never change, so the compiler holds you to it: a value you"
      | "didn't mean to change can't change by accident."
      | ""
      | "Parameters are like `let`. To change a copy, make one: `var list = items`."
      | "Changing a list (`push`, `pop`, `a[i] = ...`) needs it to be `var` too."
      | ""
      | "This is refused:"
      | ""
      | "fn main() {"
      | "    let total = 1"
      | "    total += 1"
      | "    print(total)"
      | "}"
      | ""
      | "This works:"
      | ""
      | "fn main() {"
      | "    var total = 1"
      | "    total += 1"
      | "    print(total)"
      | "}"
